    feat(board): SG-126 finding-fingerprint dedupe gate + one-shot backfill
    
    QA/dogfood lanes re-filed the same finding 3-8x: a fresh id against a
    recycled (or suffix-variant) title, so every create succeeded and the board
    grew while the real finding count stayed flat. Rows now carry a finding
    fingerprint — sha256hex(normalize(title) || 0x1F || normalize(reasoning)) —
    stored INSIDE the existing detail field (no new column, no migration, no
    change to any other field), with the row's original prose preserved under
    "text" and unknown keys of an object-form detail kept verbatim.
    
    - Board.Create refuses a row whose fingerprint is already OPEN and returns
      *ErrDuplicateFinding (existing id + digest) instead of appending. The
      refusal is durable: a task_evidence event lands on the EXISTING row
      ({"existing_task_id","new_id_attempted","fingerprint","evidence":
      {"run_id","ts"}}). --force still files a variant deliberately (the variant
      is fingerprinted too, so a later accidental re-file is caught). Fresh rows
      are fingerprinted at create time; --evidence-run-id records the run.
    - normalize(): lowercase, strip a leading recycled QA-/DF-/DOGFOOD-<PROJ>-N
      token, collapse whitespace, strip trailing (recurrence N) / (Nth) /
      [run-id-N] markers; a leading priority tag stays part of the identity, so
      an escalation is a distinct finding.
    - Both live reasoning shapes are handled: plain string, and the qa-dagger
      object form {"note": ...}.
    - The import/raw path is untouched: exported rows still land byte-for-byte,
      so export/import round-trip semantics are unchanged.
    - OpenFindingStatuses = pending, in_progress, dispatched, blocked, review.
      Anything else (complete, failed, duplicate, an unknown spelling) never
      suppresses a create — the gate fails open.
    - OpenFingerprints() (read.go) exposes id -> stored digest for the gate;
      task_evidence joins the event-type vocabulary.
    
    cmd/dedupe-board: the one-shot backfill for the duplicates already on disk.
    DRY-RUN by default, ONE board per run (--board-dir is required; there is no
    fleet sweep), --lanes is a guard that refuses any other lane (exit 2). Per
    collision group it keeps the EARLIEST row, closes the rest with
    worker_summary "merged into <kept>: dedupe backfill <date>", carries the
    merged rows' evidence onto the kept row, and appends one audit event per
    group. Group members are selected by LINE INDEX, not by id: live QA boards
    repeat an id across several lines (11 of the 11 merged-away rows on
    hivemind-work are same-id rows), so an id-keyed selection would keep both and
    merge neither. Untouched lines round-trip byte-identical (asserted before the
    write) and a second --apply is a no-op.
    
    Tests (internal/board/write_test.go): dedupe-on-fingerprint (suffix-variant
    re-file refused, existing row byte-identical, exactly one task_evidence
    event), grandfathered open row (no false match), --force override, backfill
    merge of a 3-row group (kept/merged/evidence/audit + closed and unrelated
    lines byte-identical + idempotent re-apply), dry-run byte-idempotence,
    duplicate-id collapse by line index, and normalizer / object-reasoning units.
    
    Verification: go build ./... , go vet ./... , gofmt clean, go test -short
    -count=1 ./... all green (7 new tests + 2 units). RED proof: reverting only
    the Create gate makes TestCreate_DedupeOnFingerprint and
    TestCreate_GrandfatherExistingOpenRow fail naming the missing suppression
    (TestCreate_ForceOverride still passes); restored byte-identical, then green.
    golangci-lint reports the same 49 pre-existing issues as HEAD (errcheck 42,
    staticcheck 5, unused 2) and none in the new files. Live evidence: the gate
    through the built CLI on a scratch board (exit 2 + the bare
    DUPLICATE-SUPPRESSED line on stderr, 0 new rows, 1 task_evidence event); a
    read-only dry-run sweep over 45 fleet boards found real collision groups on
    hivemind-work (9), musterflow (2), rabbit-hole (1) and hermes-canopy (1) —
    musterflow and rabbit-hole are paused lanes named in the 09-16 re-enable
    report — and fail-closed (exit 1, no writes) on the three boards that do not
    parse (consensus, ring-runner, speclang), each of which already fails
    boardctl validate at HEAD.
    
    Addresses SCHED-GAP-126.
    
